@page "/structure"

<CatHead Title="Page structure">
    The parts that hold a page together: the title row, section breaks and editorial asides.
</CatHead>

<CatGroup Title="Page head">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Structure.PageHead" Title="Title and actions" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            Heading, an optional supporting line, and the actions that apply to the whole page. It
            wraps rather than shrinking the title, so on a narrow screen the actions drop below the
            heading, at the end edge, instead of truncating it.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Structure.PageHeadFold" Title="Actions folded into a menu" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <code>.page-head-actions--fold</code> folds the actions into one menu button when the head is
            narrower than 40rem &mdash; its own width, not the window&rsquo;s, so a head in a narrow column
            folds on a wide screen too. Write the buttons and the menu both: below the step the buttons go
            and the <code>.actions-more</code> menu comes. Mark the primary action
            <code>.actions-keep</code> and it stays beside the menu. The head becomes a size container,
            so inside a flex row it needs a width of its own.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Structure.PageHeadLongLede" Title="Long supporting line" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            The text takes the room the actions leave and wraps inside it, so a supporting line of two
            sentences stays beside the button. The actions drop below only once that room is narrower
            than 20rem.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Structure.PageHeadCollapse" Title="Folding as the page scrolls" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            <strong>Live &mdash; scroll it.</strong> <code>.page-head--collapse</code> keeps the title row
            in view: over the first stretch of scrolling the supporting line folds away and the head
            takes an edge, then it stays at the top of what scrolls the page. Nothing moves under it,
            so the supporting line is held to one line. It is a scroll-driven animation and needs no
            script; where the engine has none, and for a reader who asked for reduced motion, it is
            the ordinary head and scrolls away with the page.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

<CatGroup Title="Dividers and callouts">
    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Structure.Dividers" Title="Dividers" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            A rule between sections. Use it when two blocks are related but distinct, whitespace when
            they are merely sequential, and a <a href="/card">card</a> when one of them is an object in
            its own right.
        </p>
    </CatExample>

    <CatExample TExample="Examples.Structure.Callouts" Title="Callouts" Demo="ex-demo--block">
        <p>
            An editorial aside inside prose &mdash; a note, a caveat, a worked example &mdash;
            permanent, and about the <em>content</em>. Its two neighbours are about something else:
            <a href="/alert"><code>.alert</code></a> is about the <em>page right now</em> and
            <a href="/card"><code>.card-warning</code></a> about <em>one card's own data</em>, and an
            alert that never goes away trains people to ignore alerts.
        </p>
    </CatExample>
</CatGroup>

